==English==
{{was wotd|2024|February|29}}

===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|yóh₁r̥}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*klewp-}}
From Late {{inh|en|enm|lepe-yer}}, {{m|enm|lep-yer|t=year with 366 days, leap year}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=lēp(e-yẹ̄r|pos=n|id=MED25165}}</ref> from {{m|enm|lep}}, {{m|enm|lepe|t=act of jumping, jump, leap}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=lēp|pos=''n.''(1)|noformat=1|id=MED25159}}</ref> (from {{inh|en|ang|hlīep}}, {{m|ang|hlȳp}}, probably ultimately from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*klewp-|t=to spring; to stumble}}) + {{m|enm|yer|t=calendrical unit based on a complete revolution of the Earth around the Sun, year}} (from {{inh|en|ang|ġēar}}, ultimately from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*yóh₁r̥|t=year}}). The English term is analysable as {{compound|en|leap|pos1=noun|year|notext=1|type=endocentric}}, and possibly relates to the phenomenon that any fixed date of a 365-day calendar advances one weekday each year but every date of a 366-day year after February 29 (often seen as the [[leap day]]) advances by two weekdays instead.<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=2077164979|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> For example, Christmas (December 25) fell on a Saturday in 2004, a Sunday in 2005, a Monday in 2006, and a Tuesday in 2007 but then “leapt” over Wednesday to fall on a Thursday in 2008 which was a leap year.

Compare also {{noncog|ang|mōnan hlȳp|t=moon’s leap}} and {{noncog|ML.|saltus lūnae|lit=leap moon}}, an additional day added every 19 years (a [[Metonic cycle]]) to bring the lunar and solar calendars into alignment.<ref name="OED"/>

